      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we start by finding her walking rate in miles per minute. We know that Jana takes 24 minutes to walk one mile. Therefore, her rate of walking is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Rate} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use this rate to find out how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes. We multiply her walking rate by the time she walks:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Time} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nPerforming the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\), we divide both the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12}\n\\]\n\nNow, we convert \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) to a decimal. We perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.416667\n\\]\n\nRounding this to the nearest tenth:\n\n\\[\n0.416667 \\approx 0.4\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
